# townCivic configuration. Every line here is optional.
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in only what you need — `.env` is gitignored. Nothing
# in this file is required to run: `npm install && npm run ingest && npm run
# serve` works with all of it unset, storing documents on local disk and readers
# in `data/towncivic.db`.
#
# The CLI loads `.env` from the repository root on startup, so filling this in
# is all you have to do — no `export`, no `source`.
#
# In particular, do NOT `source .env`. It looks like it works and does not:
# sourcing a file of KEY=value lines sets *shell* variables, so `echo $S3_BUCKET`
# prints the value while `npm run documents` sees nothing. (`set -a; source .env;
# set +a` is the shell spelling, if you want it in your shell too.)
#
# A real environment variable always wins over a line in this file, so a `.env`
# left in a working copy can never override what Fly, systemd or GitHub Actions
# passed in. Deployments set real variables and ignore this file entirely.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the basics

# Where the pipeline writes. Must be a volume if documents are stored locally.
# TOWNCIVIC_DATA_DIR=/var/lib/towncivic

# PORT=8787

# The origin the *server* is reachable at, for absolute links in Atom feeds and
# the personal-feed URL. The default is right for localhost; set it once there
# is a public hostname.
# TOWNCIVIC_BASE_URL=https://towncivic.example

# Marks the session cookie `Secure`. Required for anything served over HTTPS,
# and must stay off for plain-HTTP localhost or signing in appears to fail.
# TOWNCIVIC_SECURE_COOKIES=1

# ------------------------------------------------- readers (serve-time only)
#
# The pipeline never reads a user, so none of this belongs in a refresh job.
# Setup: supabase/README.md

# TOWNCIVIC_ACCOUNTS=supabase

# SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co

# The publishable key (`sb_publishable_…`) or the legacy anon key (`eyJ…`).
# Both are the same role. Never a secret or service-role key: those bypass
# row-level security, which is the only thing keeping one reader's list out of
# another's.
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=sb_publishable_...

# Optional. Not a session store — sessions live in Supabase — so changing it
# signs nobody out; it only expires forms already open in a browser. Set it to
# survive restarts, and so several instances accept each other's forms.
# TOWNCIVIC_SESSION_SECRET=

# -------------------------------------------- the document archive (pipeline)
#
# Any S3-compatible store. This is the half that cannot be regenerated, so it
# is the half worth putting somewhere durable.
# Setup: docs/operations.md#moving-the-archive-out

# TOWNCIVIC_DOCUMENTS=s3
# S3_BUCKET=towncivic

# Cloudflare R2. The account id is on the R2 overview page.
#   S3_ENDPOINT=https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
#   S3_REGION=auto
# Backblaze B2:  https://s3.<region>.backblazeb2.com
# Tigris:        https://fly.storage.tigris.dev
# MinIO:         your own host
# AWS S3:        leave S3_ENDPOINT unset and set S3_REGION to a real region.
# S3_ENDPOINT=
# S3_REGION=auto

# For R2 these come from R2 → "Manage API tokens", NOT from a Cloudflare API
# token: the S3 protocol needs a key pair, and a Cloudflare API token is a
# bearer token for a different API entirely. The secret is shown once.
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Only for temporary/STS credentials. Most setups leave this unset.
# S3_SESSION_TOKEN=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ optional

# Read only by `interpret --provider anthropic`, which is off by default.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
